{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cee2rJk7kb6EVWBv9hGDr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll summarize where things stand while the suite finishes.\n\n**Rework addressed** — the MAJOR finding was real, and the fix is a deletion rather than a guard.\n\n**Root cause (one sentence):** \"which drafted rows may the loop approve?\" was answered in two places — `intake._released_drafted_rows` filtered by the dial, `adjudicate_brief.first_approval_values` re-resolved the population live and never put that filter back.\n\nThe rung table existed *twice* for one commit — `intake.APPROVAL_RUNG` (registry-keyed) and `adjudicate_brief._APPROVAL_RUNG_OF` (tier-keyed, used only by the amendment aftermath) — and the first-approval brief, the third consumer, was wired to neither. Both copies are gone; `agent_common.SPINE_APPROVAL_RUNGS` + `human_approves_spine` is the one home, beside the off-spine `APPROVAL_RUNGS`/`human_approves` pair it mirrors arm for arm. `intake.py` and `adjudicate_brief.py` both shrank.\n\nBecause the filter is now in the derivation, not the prose: a held row still appears in the chain (labelled `HELD FOR THE OWNER, NOT YOURS TO FLIP`) but contributes no registry to `--approves`; an SR with no released Drafted row is dropped whole; an emptied population refuses, naming the dial.\n\n**Why the suite missed it:** `_first_approval_repo` declared no `docs/process.toml`, so its dial fell back to `DevStg-Release` — the fixture wasn't testing this arm at all. It now declares `DevStg-Needs`, plus a new test driving the mixed `DevStg-Reqs` dial end to end, and a structural pin on the new table mirroring its off-spine twin.\n\n**One part of the finding I did not take, named rather than skipped silently:** carrying the minted row identities as typed scope. `_draft_row` serializes only `wi_convert.COLUMNS` and `parse_spec` drops non-column frontmatter, so a typed scope carrier means a new registry column every adopting repo migrates to. Against that, the module's stated rule is that a brief re-derives its population rather than remembering the mint's, and the adjudication lane is exclusive on trunk. The finding's failure scenario turns on the dial, which is now filtered from one home at both ends. Reasoning is in the fragment under \"Not taken, and why\" — if the owner wants identity-scoping, it's a schema row of its own.\n\nThree commits landed, each through the commit-bar hooks (smoke 1453 passed / 1 failed, 21.15 s against a 60 s budget). The one red is the pre-existing `test_wi_convert` README-fence failure this fragment already documents as reproducing at `9eaeac93`, trunk before this WI was claimed — not mine. I'll report the full unfiltered suite numbers when it lands, then re-run at final HEAD before calling this done."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":919,"cache_read_input_tokens":199993,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":919},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"25d61717-b3c5-49db-8ea6-5370fce9aec1","uuid":"80c6d70c-d36a-42c3-97b9-1a010d77a91e","request_id":"req_011Cee2rHtGwhrigP4sRZWh1"}
